From 8f30f7391246a826acf3a9a041f2d84bb07d32c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 14:04:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(preview): stop framing a URL Traefik can only 502,
 and take npm install off the critical path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two separate causes of "Bad Gateway" in the preview pane, and both came from
treating "the container exists" as "the app is serving".

  1. runProject waited for the dev server, and then returned ok:true whether or
     not it ever answered. The route persisted phase "ready", the editor framed
     the URL, and Traefik — which always answers — served 502 into the iframe.
  2. reconnect() checked only that the container was Running. Its pid 1 is
     `sleep infinity`, so it stays Running through a vite crash, an OOM of the
     dev server, and the whole cold-start window. That call is the editor's
     first on every open, so a warm-but-not-serving sandbox put Bad Gateway on
     screen at the moment the user arrived.

Both now report readiness honestly via SandboxRunResult.ready, and the URL is
withheld until a probe confirms it. A boot that hasn't finished parks at phase
"starting"; the phaseOnly poll promotes it to ready the instant a probe
actually succeeds (only "verified" counts — peekPreviewReachable fails open, so
its true also means "never checked"). reconnect answers `waking: true` so the
client can tell "needs a moment" from "no sandbox" and doesn't cold-boot a
container that is seconds from answering.

Readiness is now measured INSIDE the container instead of through the tunnel.
The old probe fetched the public https hostname from the app server, which made
boot time depend on ACME: Traefik obtains a per-project certificate on first use
via HTTP-01, and until it lands an HTTPS fetch throws a TLS error the probe
cannot distinguish from "vite isn't listening" — so an app serving in 15s could
still burn the full 120s budget. `wget http://127.0.0.1:PORT/` over exec answers
in milliseconds, and requesting `/` rather than just opening a socket starts
Vite's dependency pre-bundling during boot instead of on the user's first paint.

Speed, beyond the probe: `npm install` gains --prefer-offline and quiet output,
and the provider now feature-detects /opt/lm-base/node_modules and hardlinks it
in (`cp -al`) so the install becomes a delta reconcile. docker/sandbox/ builds
that image — ~340 packages, npm cache deliberately retained — and its
package.json is generated from the same source the app generator uses, so a
version bump can't silently drift it. Boots on any other image are unchanged.

Also: keepAlive no longer condemns a sandbox when only the tunnel is failing.
It falls back to the local probe, and if the app answers reports healthy with
restarted:true — the iframe may be stuck on an error page, so it still gets
reloaded, but the container is not destroyed and cold-booted underneath a
working app. And the paint watchdog goes 6s -> 12s: it was reloading iframes
that were mid-first-paint, discarding the optimizer's progress and making the
preview slower in exactly the case it existed to rescue.

A boot that stalls at "starting" gets one cold boot, then surfaces the dev log
rather than spinning forever.

tsc --noEmit: 216 errors before, 216 after, identical set. Production build passes.
